Z||000002||TooLowFiero||09-30-1999||07:03 PM||||yeah i checked everything out and the clutch works fine   but it doesnt like to go in gear  what else could it be<P>TooLow||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||09-30-1999||07:29 PM||||If 3rd and 4th check out ok, then its quite likely just a shift linkage problem.  Pop off the console and check the cables and cable retaining clips.  Check for free movement there and at the trans.  You may be want to get a freind to help with the trans observations.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000004||WGraffiKs||09-30-1999||07:52 PM||||My shop manual suggests to perform a linkage alignment when these symptoms occur.  Two pins are placed in the linkage under the shift console, then two bolts are loosened and adjusted at the linkage entering the tranny.  I assume you have a 4-speed, and that all this applies to your transmission.  E-mail me if you want more info on the procedure.  I've done it before, and it does make a difference, but you sometimes have to repeat it to get the shift feel you want.Good thing it's an easy procedure to do!||||reg||1
Z||000005||TooLowFiero||10-01-1999||12:48 AM||||ok  so all i have to do is realign my shifting linkage????  i drove it again tonight and now all the gears are a pain in the butt to get into and shifting into reverse slams pretty hard i can now get it into first gear ok  but have to slam it into second  then into third and fourth    yes it is a 4 speed and this is starting to upset me i may have only paid 100 bucks for the car but it has a rebuilt motor new clutch and new tires plus ive been bitten by the fiero bug i have an 86 2m4 and now an 84 se coupe all i want is for this thing to shift smooth  please help  and please give me tips on how to realign my linkage   thanks guys<P><BR>TooLow||||reg||5
Z||000006||DJRice||10-01-1999||09:24 AM||||I hate to say this but if you just bought the car...for only $100...and it has a new clutch and new tires..then someone wanted to get rid of that car pretty bad because they did not trust it.  I am wondering if your new clutch was installed properly???<P>However, I would double check the linkages and shifter itself before I started thinking about major tranny repairs.  I bought a car with a supposed bad tranny that was just missing a retaining clip on the <B>brand new</B> shifter cable.  The guy didnt replace the retainer after replacing the cable and just assumed that the transmission was screwed because he only had second gear.  I didnt feel bad at all about taking the car off his hands because I could hear and feel the cable smacking the underside of the console.    ||||reg||1
Z||000007||TooLowFiero||10-01-1999||10:22 AM||||well i the clutch checked out  its A-OK  and i replaced the selector and shift cable myself  and retainer pins are there  could it be the tranny?? damn i hope not thats the one thing we dont repair at my work and how do i re-align my linkage??<P>TooLow||||reg||9
Z||000008||TOM||10-01-1999||12:16 PM||||Is your clutch pedal aluminum? Most are and they bend. Causing the clutch master not to be pushed in enough for smooth shifting. You need to get a steel pedal. They are cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Greg Piet||10-01-1999||08:53 PM||||TooLow:<BR>Is it hard to shift when the engine is off?<BR>if not, but it is a bear to shift when the engine is running it is the clutch system.<BR>I had this problems and I did 3 things.<BR>1) rebuild master cylinder (it didnt leak)<BR>2) rebulid/replace slave cylinder (it didnt leak either, but it does have that nylon piece on the end.<BR>3) the clutch pedal has a pressed in shaft which went through the master cylinder pushrod.  The shaft was extremely worn, which added 1/4 to 1/2 an inch play.  I pressed out the old shaft and pressed in a new one.  <P>My rebuild order was 1 3 2  where 1 did nothing, 3 improved shifting and 2 fixed it! And remember that the clutch bleeding can be a pain as well.<P>PS: the isuzu trannys are a bit klunky to begin with. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||88 Fiero||10-02-1999||12:44 AM||||TooLow...Tom is right..you better check your clutch pedal and see if it's bent. Keith of Fiero Motorsports said that the Fiero '84 to '86 have a defect on the clutch pedal. Pontiac did not issue a recall on this and just advice the dealer that if someone complains just replace it with the correct ones. Also he said there's nothing wrong with your tranny. Through his experience..The reverse gear not be affected if so just minor. And if all the gears are not shifting correctly...he said most probable the push rod of the slave cylinder or the bent pedal..Even the bent of the pedal is 1/8 it will make a big difference when you push it because it's not going all the way in...So check your pedal or better yet...just replace it....||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||10-02-1999||01:51 AM||||I have a couple questions on this matter.. sort of.<P>where exactly is the clutch itself in a Fiero? Second.. I only know a bit about manual trans cars, so I hope this doesn't sound like a stupid question..<P>to engage the clutch, the pedal is pushed down, and it pulls a plate, making the friction pads (don't know what they're actually called) pull away from other friction pads(??) so the gears can shift.. is this what happens? If not, can anyone explain it to me?<P>thanks!!||||reg||5
Z||000012||lowCG||10-02-1999||03:29 AM||||Clutch is on the back(non-fan belt)end of the motor,facing the driver's side,inside the first part of the transmission just under where it bolts to the motor.<BR>The flywheel spins,bolted to the crankshaft on the rear of the motor,and has the clutch mechanism bolted to it.<BR>The flywheel has one friction surface(smooth steel,like a brake rotor),the clutch pressure plate has another surface like the flywheel,trapped between the two surfaces,and squeezed by spring pressure is a disk.The disk has a splined,or slotted hole in the center where the shaft from the transmission goes through.When the clutch pedal is pressed,the disk is freed from the moving pressure plate/flywheel by the release of the springs that normally hold tight.||||reg||1
Z||000013||DaRkLoRD||10-02-1999||03:57 AM||||Hmm. thanks! I think I get it, but it's almost 4 am.. anyone got a picture?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'm working on a project to design a car (based on the Fiero frame, of course!) that has a computer controlled clutch. sort of. I'm looking to use an EM actuator to engage the clutch instead of a clutch pedal, just trying to get info on the clutch to see where the actuator would fit.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>this is all theoretical of course, I couldn't possibly afford to build this car. Not for a few years at least.||||reg||1
