Z||000016||ChadMan||10-04-1999||12:14 PM||||DarkLord, your project sounds interesting.  You might want to do some research into the system used in the Ferrari F355 F1 and the new F360 Modena.  Both cars use a conventional clutch and transaxle which are operated by an electromechanical system.  The driver shifts gears via paddles behind the steering wheel and the computer-controlled system executes a perfect shift in less than 1/4 second.  It even blips the throttle on downshifts, turning any driver into Michael Schumacher!  Damn, that's cool.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Carrolles||10-04-1999||05:48 PM||||When the clutch was replaced did the flywheel get re-surfaced?<P>There is very little extra travel in the slave cylinder and if the flywheel was re-surfaced you might not have enough travel left.  When a flywheel is resurfaced in a Fiero shims should be installed between the crankshaft and the flywheel to make up the lost thickness of the flywheel.<P>Hope this helps,<BR>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000018||DaRkLoRD||10-04-1999||06:55 PM||||ChadMan,it'll be more interesting if I actually get it built in a few years.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My idea is to drill a hole in the center of a manual trans shifter, and put an automatic shift knob on, from a Cavalier or some other car with the button on top. A switch would be installed in the knob, and wires run down thru the manual shifter. When you hit the button on the shifter, it engages the clutch, you shift, and the clutch disengages when you let go of the button. Should be simple. I emailed the people at Aura Systems Inc. about their EM actuators, but no reply yet. I'm trying to find out how much force their biggest actuator puts out, to see if it's strong enough to engage a high performance clutch like the Centerforce dual friction one.<p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 10-04-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||lowCG||10-04-1999||08:22 PM||||Volkswagen(late sixties,aircooled)automatics worked like that,might be worth looking at.<BR>Friend had one with a little clutch button on the shifter,he could really throw that car around too.||||reg||1
Z||000020||batmobile||10-06-1999||12:45 PM||||Darklord:<BR>Two types of automated manuals out there.  One is manual transmission with electronically-controlled hydraulic actuators to perform clutch apply and shifting. Offered by BMW (M3 sports car) and Ferrari.<P>Second is clutch-less manual that uses electronically-controlled hydraulic actuators to perform clutch applies.  Driver manipulates conventional manual shift lever. Offered by Saab (Sensonic), Renault (Zoom), Alpina (B10), Mercedes (A-Class), and MCC Smart.<P>So... if you want to do something new and different, try another.<P>Continuously variable transmissions are fun.  There are a bunch of transverse designs that could be retro'd in.<P>Good wrenching.<BR>||||reg||1
